    raw_hard = [("Under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, what are the statutory criteria for designating a 'Significant Data Fiduciary' (SDF)?", 'Factors evaluated by the Central Govt: volume/sensitivity of data, risk of harm, sovereignty/integrity of India, and public order', ['A company that has paid more than Rs 100 crore in corporate income taxes', 'Any enterprise that employs more than 1,000 foreign software engineers', 'A corporation that is listed on both BSE and NASDAQ stock exchanges'], 'Section 10 mandates that SDFs must appoint an in-house DPO, an independent data auditor, and conduct Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIA).'), ('Under Section 9 of the DPDP Act, 2023, what strict obligations are imposed on processing the personal data of children?', 'Mandatory verifiable parental consent, prohibition on behavioral tracking/targeted ads directed at children, and no harm to child well-being', ['A flat fee of Rs 500 payable to the central data board per child user', 'A complete statutory ban on children using any computer hardware', 'Child data can be processed freely without any parental notification'], 'The Act safeguards minors (below 18 years) against predatory corporate tracking, profiling, and targeted digital advertising.'), ('What is the legal status of cross-border personal data transfers under Section 16 of the DPDP Act, 2023?', 'Transfers are permitted to all countries EXCEPT those explicitly blacklisted/restricted by the Central Government via negative list', ['Data cannot leave Indian physical borders under any circumstances (100% localization)', 'Transfers are permitted only to countries that share a land border with India', 'Data can only be transferred to international sovereign United Nations servers'], "The Act adopted a 'blacklisting/negative list' approach, facilitating global digital trade while preserving sovereign regulatory intervention power."), ("In the Supreme Court landmark ruling 'K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India' (2017), privacy was declared a fundamental right under which Article?", 'Article 21 (Protection of Life and Personal Liberty) as part of the golden triangle with Articles 14 and 19', ['Article 32 (Right to Constitutional Remedies)', 'Article 19(1)(g) (Right to Practice any Profession)', 'Article 300A (Right to Property)'], 'The unanimous 9-judge bench established that privacy is an intrinsic, inalienable fundamental right encompassing informational self-determination.'), ('In the Puttaswamy-I (Privacy, 2017) judgment, what is the three-fold proportionality test established for any state interference with privacy?', 'Legality (backed by valid law), Legitimate State Aim, and Proportionality (least intrusive means with adequate safeguards)', ['Executive convenience, political popularity, and low administrative cost', 'Cabinet approval, police recommendation, and international treaty compliance', 'Public referendum, judicial consensus, and presidential proclamation'], 'Any privacy infringement must pass the three-pronged standard: statutory authority, legitimate purpose, and proportional necessity.'), ("In 'K.S. Puttaswamy (Aadhaar, 2018)' judgment, which Section of the Aadhaar Act, 2016 was struck down as unconstitutional?", 'Section 57 (insofar as it permitted private corporations to mandate Aadhaar authentication for commercial contracts)', ['Section 3 (issuance of Aadhaar numbers to residents)', 'Section 7 (mandating Aadhaar for subsidies and welfare benefits)', 'Section 28 (security and confidentiality of biometric data)'], 'The Constitution Bench held that commercial exploitation of Aadhaar by private telecom and banks violated individual privacy.'), ("In the 'Shreya Singhal v. Union of India' (2015) judgment, which Section of the Information Technology Act was struck down completely?", 'Section 66A (criminalizing sending offensive messages via computers, held vague and violative of Article 19(1)(a))', ['Section 69A (blocking websites in national security interest)', 'Section 79 (intermediary safe harbor provisions)', 'Section 43A (compensation for failure to protect data)'], 'The Supreme Court struck down Section 66A for chilling free speech and suffering from the vice of unconstitutional overbreadth.'), ("In Shreya Singhal (2015), how did the Supreme Court 'read down' Section 79(3)(b) regarding intermediary content takedowns?", 'Intermediaries lose safe harbor only if they fail to take down content after receiving an official Court Order or authorized Government notice', ['Intermediaries must take down content upon receiving any private citizen email complaint', 'Intermediaries are legally obligated to censor all user political opinions', 'Intermediaries can never be prosecuted under any circumstances'], 'The Court prevented private censorship, holding that platforms are not arbiters of legality; takedown duty triggers upon lawful court/state order.'), ("In 'Anuradha Bhasin v. Union of India' (2020), what landmark principle did the Supreme Court establish regarding internet shutdowns?", 'Freedom of speech and trade over the internet are fundamental rights under Article 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g); shutdowns must satisfy proportionality', ['Government has unlimited sovereign authority to shut down internet indefinitely', 'Internet access is a luxury service with zero constitutional status', 'Internet shutdowns can be ordered verbally without any written record'], 'The Court ruled that indefinite suspension of internet services is illegal, mandating periodic review and publication of suspension orders.'), ('What are the statutory requirements under the Temporary Suspension of Telecom Services (Public Emergency or Public Safety) Rules, 2017?', 'Suspension orders must be issued by the Union/State Home Secretary, recorded in writing, and reviewed by a Review Committee within 5 days', ['Any police constable can order an internet shutdown at their own discretion', 'Shutdown orders can remain in place for up to 10 years without review', 'Orders must be approved by the Supreme Court Chief Justice beforehand'], 'The 2017 Rules provide procedural safeguards against arbitrary, unauthorized local internet cutoffs by junior police officers.'), ("What is 'Zero Trust Architecture' (ZTA) mandated in enterprise e-governance cybersecurity guidelines?", "'Never trust, always verify': requiring strict identity verification and least-privilege access for every user and device, inside or outside the network", ['Assuming all internal employees are completely trustworthy without passwords', 'Installing a single hardware firewall at the perimeter of the secretariat', 'Allowing public unrestricted access to all internal departmental servers'], 'ZTA eliminates implicit trust based on network location, segmenting resources and continuously verifying session credentials.'), ("What is 'Microservices Architecture' in modern e-governance platform engineering (e.g., CoWIN, ONDC)?", 'Decomposing an application into a collection of small, independently deployable, loosely coupled services communicating via APIs', ['Building one massive monolithic codebase stored on a single central server', 'Running software exclusively on offline desktop personal computers', 'Using paper index cards to route computational server tasks'], 'Microservices enable massive scalability, isolated failure blast radiuses, and continuous independent deployment of feature components.'), ("What is 'OAuth 2.0' and 'OpenID Connect' (OIDC) utilized for in national single sign-on (NSSO / MeriPehchaan)?", 'OAuth 2.0 provides delegated authorization; OIDC adds an identity authentication layer enabling secure user login across portals', ['A cryptographic algorithm used exclusively for mining Bitcoin', 'A telecom protocol for routing 5G cellular phone voice calls', 'A database query language for retrieving municipal land titles'], 'OIDC standardizes digital identity tokens (JWT), allowing citizen authentication without exposing underlying passwords to third-party portals.'), ("What is a 'Zero-Knowledge Proof' (ZKP) and how does it advance privacy in digital public identity governance?", 'A cryptographic method enabling one party to prove a statement is true without revealing any underlying information (e.g., proving age > 18 without date of birth)', ['A technique that forces users to reveal all their personal passwords', 'A method for encrypting computer hard drives using zero keys', 'An audit process where government inspectors know zero technical code'], 'ZKP allows anonymous verification of credentials (e.g., eligibility verification) with zero leakage of private identifiable data.'), ("What is 'Differential Privacy' deployed in public statistical census and health databases?", 'Injecting calibrated mathematical noise into query results to prevent the re-identification of individual records while preserving aggregate trends', ['Restricting all citizen access to public health datasets permanently', 'Encrypting database hard drives using biometric fingerprint keys', 'Separating male and female medical records into different physical rooms'], 'Differential privacy mathematically guarantees that the inclusion or exclusion of an individual in a dataset does not meaningfully change the output.'), ("What is 'Synthetic Data' in AI and digital public health governance?", 'Artificially generated data that mimics the statistical properties of real datasets without containing any actual personal identifiable records', ['Fake fraudulent financial records submitted to evade income taxes', 'Inaccurate laboratory test results generated by broken equipment', 'Data typed manually by junior clerks without verification'], 'Synthetic data accelerates AI model training and public research in medicine and fraud detection without risking sensitive patient confidentiality.'), ("In national data infrastructure, what is 'Metadata' and why is its governance legally critical?", 'Data that describes other data (e.g., timestamps, location coordinates, caller numbers, file formats, and routing paths)', ['The physical plastic casing protecting computer hardware chips', 'The paper operating manual supplied with computer servers', 'The electricity power cables connecting data center backup generators'], 'Metadata often reveals sensitive behavioral patterns, habits, and associations as invasively as primary content, warranting strict privacy protections.'), ("In cybersecurity governance, what does 'SIEM' stand for in Security Operations Centres (SOC)?", 'Security Information and Event Management', ['System Infrastructure and Encryption Module', 'Server Identification and Emergency Monitor', 'Secure Internet Exchange Mandate'], 'SIEM software aggregates real-time event logs, correlates anomalous network patterns, and flags unauthorized intrusion attempts across public cloud systems.'), ("What is 'SOAR' in advanced cybersecurity operations?", 'Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response', ['System Optimization and Asset Recovery', 'Server Operations and Audit Reporting', 'Security Online Analysis Router'], 'SOAR automates incident response playbooks, containing compromised endpoints and blocking malicious IP addresses within seconds.'), ("What is 'Air-Gapping' in the cybersecurity of critical national infrastructure (e.g., nuclear power plants)?", 'Physically isolating a computer or network from any connection to the public internet or other unsecured external networks', ['Installing high-powered air-conditioning cooling fans in server racks', 'Using wireless Wi-Fi routers positioned high on building rooftops', 'Banning all paper documents inside control room facilities'], 'Air-gapping prevents remote external cyber exploitation, though air-gapped networks remain vulnerable to physical USB drive malware (e.g., Stuxnet).'), ('Under the IT Act, 2000, what is the legal significance of Section 65B regarding the admissibility of electronic records in court?', 'Mandates a formal certificate authenticating the source, production, and custody of electronic evidence (reaffirmed in Arjun Panditrao, 2020)', ['Abolishes all electronic evidence in criminal felony trials', 'Allows police to alter digital video evidence without justification', 'Requires all computer evidence to be printed on legal stamp paper'], 'Section 65B ensures the forensic integrity and authenticity of secondary electronic records (CDs, printouts, hard drive extracts) admitted in trials.'), ("In 'Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal' (2020), what did the Supreme Court clarify regarding Section 65B(4) certificates?", 'A Section 65B(4) certificate is an absolute condition precedent for admitting secondary electronic evidence where the original device is not produced', ['The certificate is optional and can be waived by police inspectors', 'Physical printouts can be admitted without any electronic verification', 'Electronic records cannot be admitted in election dispute cases'], 'The 3-judge bench resolved conflicting jurisprudence, holding that production of the 65B certificate is mandatory for secondary digital records.'), ("What is the role of the 'Centre of Excellence in Blockchain Technology' established by NIC?", 'Developing enterprise blockchain frameworks for tamper-evident public registries (land titles, educational degrees, supply chain, blood bank tracking)', ['Trading speculative cryptocurrencies on international exchanges', 'Mining Bitcoin using high-speed government supercomputers', 'Issuing private corporate stock shares for commercial startups'], 'NIC uses permissioned blockchain architectures (Hyperledger Fabric) to provide immutable audit trails for critical public records.'), ("What does 'Digital Public Infrastructure' (DPI) encompass according to G20 New Delhi Leaders' Declaration (2023)?", 'Shared digital systems that are secure, interoperable, inclusive, built on open standards, and catalyze delivery of public/private services at population scale', ['Privately owned closed proprietary walled-garden software ecosystems', 'Hardware computing terminals sold exclusively to government departments', 'Physical asphalt highway networks equipped with electronic toll gates'], 'The G20 consensus championed DPI as a global paradigm for financial inclusion, health equity, and sustainable development goals.'), ("In e-governance API design, what is 'REST' (Representational State Transfer)?", 'A stateless architectural style that uses standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) for communication between web systems', ['A software program that pauses computer servers to save electricity', 'A cryptographic key used to lock database access at night', 'A proprietary protocol developed exclusively for military satellites'], 'RESTful APIs enable lightweight, modular, and cross-platform communication across independent government and third-party applications.'), ("In cloud governance, what is the 'MeghRaj' (GI Cloud) initiative driven by MeitY?", 'A national government cloud initiative accelerating e-service delivery through standardized secure cloud platforms and empanelled cloud providers', ['A weather prediction cloud radar system operated by IMD in Pune', 'A sovereign foreign policy treaty on transboundary space communications', 'An emergency rainfall flood relief portal for rural farmers'], 'MeghRaj optimizes public ICT spending by sharing infrastructure, avoiding hardware duplication, and enforcing security empanelment.'), ("Under the DPDP Act, 2023, what is 'Right to Erasure' and what are its statutory exceptions?", 'Data Principals have the right to request deletion of personal data, UNLESS retention is necessary for compliance with law or specified purpose', ['Citizens can demand immediate deletion of all police criminal records', 'Platforms can refuse all deletion requests without providing reasons', 'Citizens can delete their records from national bank balance sheets'], 'Right to erasure allows individuals to retract personal data when the processing purpose is served, balanced against statutory retention mandates.'), ("Under the DPDP Act, 2023, what is 'Deemed Consent' replaced by in the enacted statutory text?", "'Certain Legitimate Uses' (e.g., voluntary provision of data, fulfilling government welfare benefits, medical emergencies, employment purposes)", ['Implied commercial consent for marketing phone calls', 'Unrestricted consent to sell data to foreign brokers', 'Verbal authorization given during casual conversations'], 'Section 7 specifies narrow legitimate grounds where personal data may be processed without explicit prior consent for essential civic functions.'), ("What is 'Adjudicating Officer' under Section 46 of the Information Technology Act, 2000?", 'An officer of Director/IT Secretary rank appointed by the state government to hold inquiries and award civil damages up to Rs 5 crore for cyber contraventions', ['A criminal court magistrate who sentences hackers to prison', 'A police officer who arrests cyber offenders on the street', 'An external private lawyer hired by corporate victims'], 'Section 46 provides a fast, quasi-judicial mechanism to award civil compensation for unauthorized data access, hacking, and virus damage.'), ("What is 'Cyber Swachhta Kendra' (Botnet Cleaning and Malware Analysis Centre) operated by CERT-In?", 'A national initiative providing free tools and botnet removal solutions to disinfect citizen and enterprise computing devices from malware', ['A municipal waste management sanitation portal in smart cities', 'A corporate training center for commercial call center workers', 'A hardware recycling facility for broken computer motherboards'], 'Cyber Swachhta Kendra collaborates with antivirus vendors and ISPs to alert citizens of botnet infections and offer automated disinfection.'), ("In digital government architecture, what is 'Interoperability' and why is it essential?", 'The capability of disparate information systems and software applications to exchange data seamlessly using open technical standards', ['The requirement that all government computers be of identical brand and color', 'The capability of a computer to run without electricity power backup', 'The rule that all government software must be written in English'], 'Interoperability prevents vendor lock-in and eliminates data silos, allowing health, transport, and finance systems to talk to each other.'), ("What does 'G2C', 'G2B', and 'G2G' stand for in e-governance service interaction classifications?", 'Government-to-Citizen, Government-to-Business, and Government-to-Government digital service delivery flows', ['General-to-Custom, General-to-Broad, and General-to-Global', 'Governance-to-Corporate, Governance-to-Bank, and Governance-to-Grid', 'Global-to-Central, Global-to-Branch, and Global-to-Government'], 'These classifications categorize public digital interactions, guiding tailored portal UI/UX, security, and integration frameworks.'), ("What is 'Citizen Charter' in contemporary digital service delivery portals?", 'A formal public commitment specifying standards of service delivery, timeframes, user rights, and grievance redressal mechanisms', ['A legal passport issued to citizens travelling across domestic states', 'A voter registration card issued prior to legislative assembly elections', 'An annual membership card granting access to government library archives'], 'Digital Citizen Charters enhance accountability by publishing exact service SLA turnaround times (e.g., issuing certificates within 7 days).')]
